                <h2>The Conclusion: Compliance Is an Ongoing Operating Routine</h2>
<p>Many overseas teams treat compliance as something that starts only after an ad is rejected. In practice, the most expensive risks often arise from inconsistencies among the ad account, payment setup, creative, and landing page.</p>
<p>For example, an ad may promise a “free trial” while the landing page requires users to add a credit card immediately. Another campaign may continue promoting an expired discount weeks after the promotion ended. These issues may not trigger an immediate suspension, but they can lead to rejections, wasted spend, lower conversion rates, and increased account risk.</p>
<p>A more effective approach is to complete a focused 30-minute audit every week and record each finding, owner, and deadline. Because platform rules vary by country, industry, and account type, the checklist should also be reviewed against the latest requirements for each target market.</p>
<h2>Six Areas to Check</h2>
<h3>1. Account Access and Permissions</h3>
<ul>
<li>Confirm the relationship among ad accounts, administrators, payment accounts, and the Business Manager.</li>
<li>Remove access for former employees and deactivate unused shared accounts.</li>
<li>Enable two-factor authentication and ensure critical operations do not depend on one employee’s email verification.</li>
<li>Review agency authorizations so company assets are not managed through personal accounts.</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Balances, Payments, and Funding</h3>
<ul>
<li>Review balances, credit limits, recent charges, and failed transactions.</li>
<li>Set balance alerts to prevent campaigns from stopping after repeated payment failures.</li>
<li>Confirm that the payment method, billing entity, and operating company are consistent.</li>
<li>Reconcile invoices, taxes, and refunds, particularly when funding multiple accounts through a central payment process.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Creatives and Ad Copy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Verify that prices, discounts, trial terms, product support, and delivery estimates remain accurate.</li>
<li>Avoid unsupported absolute claims such as “guaranteed returns,” “lowest price,” or “zero risk.”</li>
<li>Compare product names, specifications, and intended audiences across the creative, landing page, and checkout page.</li>
<li>Confirm that images, music, likenesses, and fonts are licensed for commercial use.</li>
<li>Ads in finance, healthcare, supplements, or restricted sectors should receive additional review for market-specific entry requirements and advertising restrictions.</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Landing Pages and Conversion Paths</h3>
<ul>
<li>Test the domain, SSL certificate, mobile performance, and page redirects.</li>
<li>Ensure that the privacy policy, terms of service, cookie notice, and unsubscribe option are clearly accessible.</li>
<li>Every promotional offer shown in an ad should be reproducible on the landing page.</li>
<li>Check for deceptive buttons, forced subscriptions, autoplay, or landing-page content unrelated to the advertised offer.</li>
</ul>
<h3>5. Data Collection and Privacy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ensure forms collect only the information required to provide the service.</li>
<li>Do not make public promises about data-retention periods that the company cannot enforce.</li>
<li>Reconcile event definitions across pixels, SDKs, app events, and the CRM.</li>
<li>Establish permission and expiration controls for remarketing, audience matching, and cross-platform campaigns.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. Financial Reconciliation and Evidence</h3>
<ul>
<li>Reconcile amounts through platform invoices, payment records, and accounting documents.</li>
<li>Keep the creative version, landing-page snapshot, approval record, and launch date for every ad.</li>
<li>Document rejection reasons, appeal materials, and final outcomes to identify recurring issues.</li>
</ul>
<h2>A 30-Minute Audit SOP</h2>
<p><strong>Minutes 1–5: Review account notifications.</strong> Focus on rejections, payment warnings, permission changes, policy notices, and identity-verification requests.</p>
<p><strong>Minutes 6–12: Check account health.</strong> Review balances, failed charges, unusual drops in delivery, conversion anomalies, and identical rejection reasons across multiple accounts.</p>
<p><strong>Minutes 13–22: Sample active ads.</strong> Start with high-spend, long-running campaigns and ads in sensitive categories. Compare each creative with its landing page and current product information.</p>
<p><strong>Minutes 23–27: Resolve access and funding issues.</strong> Remove invalid administrators, replenish balances, replace failed payment methods, and pause ads with a confirmed compliance risk.</p>
<p><strong>Minutes 28–30: Document and schedule follow-up.</strong> Label findings as red, yellow, or green. Red requires immediate action or escalation; yellow requires a deadline; green means no immediate issue was found, but the check should remain documented.</p>
<h2>Anonymous Example: The Rejection May Not Start With the Creative</h2>
<p>A utility App running ads on Meta and TikTok experienced simultaneous rejections across several campaigns. The team initially assumed that a new video creative was responsible. During its weekly audit, it found that the landing page had been changed, the checkout price no longer matched the “first month free” promotion, and the payment method had failed verification.</p>
<p>The team aligned all promotional details, added weekly checks for high-spend ads, and retained versioned snapshots. Similar clustered rejections did not recur. The lesson is clear: compliance review should cover the entire path from click to payment, not only the creative inside the ad platform.</p>
<h2>Build a Continuous Tracking System</h2>
<p>A useful audit sheet should include the date, platform, account, campaign, ad ID, risk type, issue, evidence link, owner, status, and follow-up date. Recurring rejection reasons should then be converted into creative-review templates and pre-launch landing-page checklists.</p>
<p>The purpose of a compliance audit is not to guarantee that an account will never be restricted. It is to identify inconsistencies earlier, limit losses, and ensure that every action is supported by evidence. For teams managing multiple ad accounts, a consistent inspection schedule, standardized naming, and centralized documentation are often more valuable than producing additional creatives.</p>
